1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Links:==
2 CONT
2 CONT *[http://www.geneall.net/I/per_page.php?id=1695 Geneall]
2 CONT *'''Wikipedia:''' [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ludovico_II_Gonzaga,_Marquis_of_Mantua English ] [http://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ludovico_III_Gonzaga Italiano] [http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luigi_III._Gonzaga Deutsch]
2 CONT *'''Marquis of Mantua:''' Reign: 1444 -1478
2 CONT >'''Predesessor:'''  [http://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000003827455161 Gianfrancesco I] '''Successor:'''  [http://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000006727827834 Federico I]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Ludovico Gonzaga, condottiere and court ruler of Mantua, turned his castle named the Corte into a luxurious showplace for the visit of Pope Pius II in 1459. The most impressive room in Gonzaga's Mantua castle was the Sala del Pis
2 CONC anello, a great hall decorated with murals in an Arthurian theme by Pisanello in 1447-48. Mantua was home to a few holy relics, primary among them the "Most Precious Blood" and the lance of Longinus. The blood was supposedly colle
2 CONC cted from the lance which was used to pierce the side of Christ at his crucifixion.
2 CONT Gonzaga initiated a campaign to improve Mantua's appearance after the pope visited his court and reportedly criticized certain aspects of the city. The court architect Leon Batista Alberti advised Ludovico on restoration of Mantua
2 CONC  sites which housed the holy relics. Also foremost in these efforts was Andrea Mantegna who served the Gonzaga for forty-six years as court artist and was knighted by Gonzaga in the 1480s. Mantegna became an accomplished courtier 
2 CONC and so revered was that he dominated the artistic scene of Mantua for many years."
2 CONT [http://www.finearttouch.com/Ludovico_Gonzaga,_The_Court_of_Mantua.html]
2 CONT ---------------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Ludovico Gonzaga (today known as Ludovico III., but because he was the first Margrave of Mantua with this name he was called "Ludovico or Ludovico I.") , Margrave of Mantua, with his approximately five- or six-year-old grandson G
2 CONC ian Francesco II., the future Margrave of Mantua
2 CONT Born: 5 June 1412
2 CONT Deceased: 12 June 1478
2 CONT Father: Gianfrancesco I. Gonzaga (1395-1444), since 1433 Margrave of Mantua
2 CONT Mother: Paola Malatesta (1393-1449), daughter of Malatesta IV. Malatesta, Signore di Pesaro, and his wife Elisabetta da Varano
2 CONT Siblings:
2 CONT his brother Carlo Gonzaga, born between 1413 and 1423, deceased in 1456, since 1437 married to Lucia d'Este (1419-1437), daughter of Niccolò III. d'Este
2 CONT his brother Alessandro Gonzaga, born 1415 or 1427, deceased in 1466, married to Agnes da Montefeltro († 1447), daughter of Guidantonio da Montefeltro, Count of Urbino
2 CONT his sister Margherita Gonzaga (1418-1439), since 1435 married to Leonello d'Este (1407-1450)
2 CONT his brother Gianlucido Gonzaga (1421-1448)
2 CONT his sister Cecilia Gonzaga (1425-1451), nun at Santa Chiara in Mantua
2 CONT his sister Leonella Gonzaga, nun
2 CONT Spouse:
2 CONT Barbara of Brandenburg (1422-1481), first child of Margrave John of Brandenburg and his wife Barbara of Sachsen-Wittenberg; marriage on 12 November 1433
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT his son Federigo, born around 1438 (?), deceased before 1441
2 CONT his daughter Maddalena, born around 1439, deceased as infant
2 CONT his daughter Elisabetta, born around 1440, deceased as infant
2 CONT his son Federigo (I.) (1441-1484), Margrave of Mantua
2 CONT his son Francesco (1444-1483), Cardinal
2 CONT his daughter Paola Bianca (1445-1447)
2 CONT his son Gianfrancesco (1446-1496), Count of Sabbioneta and Lord of Bozzolo; married to Antonia del Balzo
2 CONT his daughter Susanna (1447-1481), nun at Santa Paola in Mantua
2 CONT his daughter Dorotea (1449-1467), married to Galeazzo Maria Sforza, Duke of Milan
2 CONT his daughter Cecilia (1451-1472/78), nun at Santa Chiara in Mantua
2 CONT his son Rodolfo (1452-1495), Lord of Castiglione delle Stiviere, Solferino, Suzzara and Poviglio; first married to Antonia Malatesta, then to Caterina Pico
2 CONT his daughter Barbara (1455-1503), since 1474 married to Eberhard I., Duke of Württemberg
2 CONT his son Lodovico (or Ludovico) (1460-1511), Bishop of Mantua
2 CONT his daughter Paola (1463-1497), married to Leonhard, Count of Gorizia"
2 CONT [https://www.kleio.org/en/history/famtree/vip/638a/]
2 CONT ------------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/ludovico-iii-gonzaga-marchese-di-mantova_(Dizionario-Biografico) 
2 CONT
2 CONT LUDOVICO III Gonzaga, Marquis of Mantua
2 CONT "The eldest son of Gianfrancesco, Marquis of Mantua, and Paola Malatesta, he was born in 1412; the sources give uncertain indications on the day and month, oscillating between 5 June by Bonamente Aliprandi and 5 July by Antonio Ne
2 CONC rli (p. 14); scholars agree for June 5. From 1423 L. and the brothers Carlo, Alessandro and Gianlucido and the sisters Margherita and Cecilia were educated in the Ca' Zoiosa at the school of Vittorino da Feltre.
2 CONT It was a pedagogical experience of the highest level, in which the young Gonzagas participated together with a conspicuous series of fellow students, both Mantuan and foreign, from which prominent politicians, prelates and humanis
2 CONC ts emerged. In fact, Vittorino Rambaldoni, who arrived in Mantua after having taught in Padua and Venice, was a very particular figure of educator. The intellectual development of his pupils, based on the learning of Latin and Gre
2 CONC ek, dialectics and rhetoric, philosophy, mathematics and music, was in fact not separated in his doctrine from a harmonious development of the body and from an education ethics to balance, to frugality, to a correct Christian feel
2 CONC ing. This educational experience was shared among all those deserving of it, regardless of rank: in this sense, the children of the marquises grew up soberly and in contact with companions of various origins, with whom they often 
2 CONC maintained significant relationships throughout their lives. In particular L., who we know was clumsy, awkward in his movements and tending towards fatness by Francesco Prendelacqua, disciple and biographer of Vittorino, was gradu
2 CONC ally educated to control his own lifestyle, accustoming his body to a parsimonious discipline that also characterized him in mature years. For L., it was a teaching of great weight: thanks to Vittorino, the future marquis develope
2 CONC d not only a balanced and rigorous education, but first-rate intellectual abilities and a particular love for the humanities. with whom they often maintained significant relationships throughout their lives. In particular L., who 
2 CONC we know was clumsy, awkward in his movements and tending towards fatness by Francesco Prendelacqua, disciple and biographer of Vittorino, was gradually educated to control his own lifestyle, accustoming his body to a parsimonious 
2 CONC discipline that also characterized him in mature years. For L., it was a teaching of great weight: thanks to Vittorino, the future marquis developed not only a balanced and rigorous education, but first-rate intellectual abilities
2 CONC  and a particular love for the humanities. with whom they often maintained significant relationships throughout their lives. In particular L., who we know was clumsy, awkward in his movements and tending towards fatness by Frances
2 CONC co Prendelacqua, disciple and biographer of Vittorino, was gradually educated to control his own lifestyle, accustoming his body to a parsimonious discipline that also characterized him in mature years. For L., it was a teaching o
2 CONC f great weight: thanks to Vittorino, the future marquis developed not only a balanced and rigorous education, but first-rate intellectual abilities and a particular love for the humanities. he was gradually educated to control his
2 CONC  own regimen of life, accustoming his body to a sparing discipline that characterized him even in his mature years. For L., it was a teaching of great weight: thanks to Vittorino, the future marquis developed not only a balanced a
2 CONC nd rigorous education, but first-rate intellectual abilities and a particular love for the humanities. he was gradually educated to control his own regimen of life, accustoming his body to a sparing discipline that characterized h
2 CONC im even in his mature years. For L., it was a teaching of great weight: thanks to Vittorino, the future marquis developed not only a balanced and rigorous education, but first-rate intellectual abilities and a particular love for 
2 CONC the humanities.
2 CONT L. in the early thirties began to prove himself as a leader: the first news we have of an independently stipulated conduct dates back to 1432. At the time the Marquis Gianfrancesco was second in command of the Venetian army under 
2 CONC the orders of Francesco Bussone, count of Carmagnola: in the spring of that year L. was hired personally for six months plus six "respect" with a contingent of 200 lances, of which 50 were under his direct authority and the remain
2 CONC ing 150 responded to more expert Gonzaga captains. It can be hypothesized that L. remained in this condition as a salaried employee of the Serenissima until at least 1436, also considering the fact that in January 1433 his father 
2 CONC had overcome his delays and had agreed to succeed Carmagnola, executed on 5 May 1432,
2 CONT Between 1432 and 1433 an event of considerable importance took place in the history of the Mantuan state: the Emperor Sigismund erected the Gonzaga domain as a marquisate, allowing Gianfrancesco to transmit the state to his sons a
2 CONC ccording to the natural order of succession. In the context of the policy of prestige and power pursued by Gianfrancesco with this title, obtained at the considerable price of 12,000 Rhenish florins, the marriage of L., stipulated
2 CONC  and celebrated in the same 1433, with Barbara of Hohenzollern, daughter of Giovanni Margrave of Brandenburg and nephew of the Elector Palatine Frederick.
2 CONT The importance that the Gonzagas attributed to this union is demonstrated by the nuptial agreements, according to which the Hohenzollern received the dowry not from her father, but from her future father-in-law: Gianfrancesco, in 
2 CONC fact, promised to deliver 25,000 Rhenish florins to some German merchants in Venice because to take them to the margrave and assigned an equivalent sum to Barbara, as wife of his son, on the income of Peschiera and Ostiglia. The a
2 CONC ge of the spouses was very different, Barbara having been born, in all probability, in September 1422; the young princess, who did not speak a word of Italian, entered Mantua on 22 November. 1433, she was first entrusted, probably
2 CONC  as a private pupil since she is not among her ordinary pupils, to Vittorino da Feltre. The marriage was consummated after the bride's twelfth birthday,
2 CONT During 1436 L. took a turn that had unforeseen consequences: in fact, apparently without Gianfrancesco's knowledge, he passed into the service of the Duke of Milan Filippo Maria Visconti with a contingent of 300 lances, signing a 
2 CONC one-year contract plus a year of "respect", with the sole proviso not to fight against the father.
2 CONT The causes of this escape and its very nature are not clear: the historiography of Mantua traces the break on the part of L. both to a desire for greater autonomy and to a deep-rooted jealousy towards his younger brother, Carlo. I
2 CONC t is not even clear whether the whole operation was actually carried out without the father's knowledge: what seems certain is that Gianfrancesco pretended to be very angry with his eldest son because of the ambiguous situation in
2 CONC  which his behavior had placed him towards his suspicious Venetians. The marquis in fact asked the emperor - and obtained - to be able to alter the order of succession to the marquisate (Nov. 3, 1436), appointing his son Carlo as 
2 CONC his heir, and banished L. from Mantua, raging against him and his daughter-in-law Barbara remained at court
2 CONT The whole affair was actually the prelude to Gianfrancesco's passage into the service of the Duke of Milan in 1438, with a sensational about-face of which L.'s conduct had perhaps been a sort of anticipation. The two Gonzagas reco
2 CONC nciled, according to tradition thanks to Paola Malatesta and Vittorino da Feltre, in 1441 and the order of succession was re-established. In the same year Barbara gave L. the longed-for heir, Federico.
2 CONT He was the first of a long series of children of which ten, between males and females (Federico, Cardinal Francesco, Gianfrancesco, Rodolfo and the prothonotary Ludovico, Susanna, Dorotea, Cecilia, Barbara and Paola), reached adul
2 CONC thood. L. also had at least two natural daughters, Gabriella, later wife of Corrado da Fogliano, and Caterina, wife of Francesco Secco.
2 CONT The passage of the Gonzagas to the Visconti front in 1438 did not bring the hoped-for territorial increases that had been the basis of Gianfrancesco's choice: the Venetian-Viscount wars of those years, on the contrary, led hostili
2 CONC ties directly into Mantua territory and ended with the peace of Cavriana of 1441, with which Mantua definitively lost Asola, Lonato and Peschiera, and with them the longed-for access to Lake Garda. In September 1444, on his death,
2 CONC  L. III succeeded his father.
2 CONT Thanks to the paternal testamentary choice, the territory controlled directly by L. III included the central body of the Marquisate without the fifteenth-century territorial increases along the western borders, a good part of the 
2 CONC Oltrepò around Gonzaga and Luzzara, and a series of vicariates in the northern part of the state such as Volta Mantovana and Cavriana, which ended up in the hands of the brothers Carlo, Gianlucido and Alessandro, who held them iur
2 CONC e fiefdoms from Ludovico III. The latter therefore inherited a state tried by decades of war, impoverished by excessive spending, reduced in the territory by the division implemented by his father in favor of the cadets and insert
2 CONC ed in an international political context which in the following ten years would be characterized by great instability.
2 CONT The five-year period from the succession of L. III (endorsed by Emperor Frederick III on 27 August 1445) to 1450 saw the new marquis enter into a series of agreements - with Milan, Florence and Venice, and with Naples - which in t
2 CONC heir close succession testify to the turmoil of the times. On 27 Sept. 1445 L. III signed a seven-year league with Filippo Maria Visconti of a purely defensive nature; since the summer of 1446, and in particular after the defeat o
2 CONC f the Visconti at Casalmaggiore on 28 September, he entered into negotiations with Florence and Venice, so much so as to negotiate with the first a conduct as captain general of the Florentine troops with 400 lances and 300 infant
2 CONC ry in time of war, 300 spears and 200 infantry in peacetime (18 Jan. 1447).
2 CONT L. III's intent was to ensure peace in the Mantua area, avoiding at all costs that the Marquisate could become a battleground or even just a passage for the opposing Venetian and Visconti sides. As part of this policy of safeguard
2 CONC ing the integrity of the Marquisate, in the spring of that same year he stipulated truces and alliances with the minor Emilian neighbors, the da Correggios, the Counts of Mirandola and Guido Torelli, Count of Guastalla.
2 CONT The death of Filippo Maria Visconti, which took place on 13 Aug. 1447, came to complicate the picture: in Milan, where the Ambrosian Republic was proclaimed, L. III's brother, Carlo, was hired by the Captains together with Frances
2 CONC co Sforza. In this phase the relations between the two brothers, albeit in opposing fields, were open and relatively good. In the spring of 1448 the effectives of the Marquis of Mantua were increased to 500 spears and 400 infantry
2 CONC . The clashes between the Sforza-Milanese army and the Venetian-Florentine army culminated on 15 September. 1448 in the battle of Caravaggio, in which L. III was forced to flee. After the agreement of Rivoltella between the Veneti
2 CONC an Republic and Sforza, L. III returned among the adherents of the Serenissima in the first months of 1449; however, the prominent position of his brother within the Ambrosian Republic did not prevent L. III from placing not only 
2 CONC himself but also his brothers Carlo and Alessandro under the protection of Venice. The situation was becoming increasingly confused: while Charles' position in Milan was becoming difficult, L. III had made agreements with Alfonso 
2 CONC of Aragon since July, confirming in November a new conduct in the king's service. L. III became lieutenant general of the Neapolitan troops in Lombardy, with a command of 900 spears and 900 infantry and with 45,000 gold florins on
2 CONC  loan, of which 30,000 would be paid immediately by the king and 15,000 by the Community of Milan; finally, the king undertook to respect the marquis's neutrality towards the Serenissima.
2 CONT The advent of Francesco Sforza to the duchy changed the terms of the picture again: in November 1450 L. III concluded with the new duke of Milan the first of a long series of contracts in which he undertook his service in case of 
2 CONC war with Venice .
2 CONT We find some now habitual clauses in the Milanese agreements, but there are also some innovations, including both the longer-than-usual duration of three years of firm plus one "of respect" and, above all, the fact that the total 
2 CONC sums to be paid to the Marquis (82,000 ducats of 54 soldi a year in wartime, 47,000 in peacetime), but the number of men-at-arms that L. III undertook to keep in service. On the contrary, it was established that the marquis undert
2 CONC ook to serve the duke "with his person, the people of arms from horse and foot and with his state and that of Mr. Alexandro his brother at every request, petition and mandate from him most illustrious duke" (State Arch. of Mantua,
2 CONC  Arch. Gonzaga, b. 51, 1 (Nov. 1450): that is, it served "for provision". This difference must certainly be traced back to a more general movement of simplification of the formal structure and terms of the conducts, but a new mean
2 CONC ing of a political and military nature also transpires from this different aspect. Here, for the first time, conduct is defined which is actually a pact of political subjection and diplomatic alliance between subjects of no equal 
2 CONC importance, within a somewhat less mobile, and therefore less open, framework than in previous decades. . In this sense, the long marquisate of L. III marked the definitive change in the military specialization of the dynasty, tog
2 CONC ether with the definition, at least until the thirties of the following century,
2 CONT In such a consolidated panorama of relations, the problem represented by Carlo Gonzaga immediately arose: Sforza, not trusting him, had him arrested and imprisoned in Binasco in the first months of 1451. L. III hurried to personal
2 CONC ly vouch for his brother , who was released on March 17, but who, far from remaining in Cerano di Lomellina where he had been confined, fled to Venice and here on February 18. 1452 stipulated a conduct under the orders of the Sere
2 CONC nissima. L. III, who had undertaken to guarantee Charles' loyalty behind the security of 80,000 ducats, found himself in the embarrassment of having to return this sum to Sforza (the question of payment, spread over eight installm
2 CONC ents of 10,000 ducats each, continued until 1459). This affair marked the end of the friendly relations between the two: L. III, who had obtained from Charles on 20 March 1451 the cession of his Mantuan lands as a guarantee of his
2 CONC  commitment to respect the agreements with the Sforza, immediately annexed them to the central body of the Marquisate. The last episode of the conflict between Milan and Venice, the war of the years 1452-54, saw the two brothers o
2 CONC penly warring in Mantua territory: Carlo, defeated in June 1453, abandoned the Marquisate and died in 1456.
2 CONT The death without heirs of Gianlucido in 1448 had already brought Volta Mantovana, Cavriana, Rodigo, Ceresara and San Martino Gusnago under the direct control of L. III: within seven years L. III had recovered almost all the terri
2 CONC tory controlled at the time by his father (the reunion became total in 1466, on the death without heirs of the last brother, Alessandro).
2 CONT The peace of Lodi (1454) marked the end of the open conflicts between Milan and Venice: the Italian League which was the direct result of it opened a period of relative stability for Mantua in the context of a Milanese alliance wh
2 CONC ich, if it cannot be defined entirely serene, she certainly remained steadfast throughout the life of Ludwig III. In the period between 1454 and 1478, the year of L. III's death, the Mediolani pacta were stipulatedin 1454, in 1459
2 CONC , in 1463, in 1466 (the latter actually stipulated with the king of Naples), in 1470 and in 1472. Among the objectives of the Italian League formed after the peace of Lodi there was also the war against the Turks: Pius II, elected
2 CONC  pope in 1458, announced a Diet of Christian princes for the following year, which took place in Mantua, the purpose of which was to organize the counteroffensive of Christianity and, if possible, the reconquest of Constantinople.
2 CONT The choice of Mantua as seat crowned the prudent policy of L. III, who had maintained a balance between the Italian powers, despite the necessary alignment alongside one of the protagonists of the Italian political scene, the Duch
2 CONC y of Milan, and which , thanks to its dynastic ties, could boast good and direct relations with the Empire and the Principalities beyond the Alps. The Diet was called for 1 June 1459: the pontiff arrived in the city on 29 May, wel
2 CONC comed with magnificence and jubilation.
2 CONT If for Pius II's purposes the Diet had to turn out to be substantially unsuccessful - a mirror of the objective difficulties in deciding and concretely promoting coordinated action by the Italian and European states against the Tu
2 CONC rks - for L. III it represented a significant personal success. In fact, on this occasion the city became for a few months (Pius II abandoned Mantua in January 1460) a sort of European capital where the representatives of the majo
2 CONC r potentates gathered.
2 CONT
2 CONT L. III took advantage of the close relations during the Diet with many high prelates, as well as the merits acquired in the eyes of the pope, to carry forward a project that was very close to his heart, the ascension of his second
2 CONC  son Francesco to the cardinalate. To this end, the Marquise Barbara managed the negotiations through the mediation of Cardinal Niccolò da Cusa: thanks to the joint pressure of Sforza, the elector Albert of Brandenburg, and the em
2 CONC peror himself, Francesco was elevated to the dignity of cardinal deacon of S. Maria Nuova on 18 Dec. 1461, despite some embarrassment caused by his young age (he was only 17).
2 CONT
2 CONT For L. III it was an event heralding both prestige and concrete authority over the Mantuan ecclesiastical bodies, even if the management of the latter sometimes caused misunderstandings and clashes between L. III and his son, caug
2 CONC ht in turn between the dynastic logics , his personal interests and the rules of the curial world. The appointment led to a strengthening of the control exercised by the family over the ecclesiastical world of Mantua: it is enough
2 CONC  to consider that the city of Mantua no longer had a bishop who was not a Gonzaga.
2 CONT
2 CONT The early Sixties were years of great importance for the Marquisate: having overcome the difficult situation of the years of succession to his father, L. III was able to organize the life of the State thanks to a period of relativ
2 CONC e stability and income which, according to expenditure administration, grew again.
2 CONT
2 CONT The institutional structure was defined in an increasingly articulated organizational chart of territorial offices and central offices: the Chancery, thanks also to the control of diplomatic activity, became an essential body for 
2 CONC the survival and functioning of the Marquisate; the Consilium domini was transformed into a real Council of Justice; the municipal farm, the rectory of revenues and the two treasuries were assigned the task of collecting and manag
2 CONC ing the financial resources of the lordship, first of all the proceeds from the Milanese pipelines. The network of territorial offices was defined in a network of 52 minor territorial districts.
2 CONT
2 CONT The relationship between the marquis and the urban political society grew in the sign of a substantial consent of the latter to the government program of the former, calibrated and prudent, but not alien to a constant and delibera
2 CONC te desire for prestige and decorum. L. III was in fact very attentive not only to the political balance, but also to the layout of the city and to the forms of urban and rural life. Between the end of the 1940s and the early 1970s
2 CONC  L. III gave life to a series of large-scale interventions on the urban fabric and in the territory, with the aim of redeveloping entire areas of the city and renewing, in a rural environment, the relationship between the agricult
2 CONC ural courts owned by the dynasty, the fortifications inherited from the previous age, the rural settlements,
2 CONT
2 CONT In this sense, L. III proved to be, as his contemporaries wrote, "a great fabricator and a very connoisseur of architecture" (Philarete), so much so as to leave scholars to suppose not only that he competently used a large group o
2 CONC f , but that he himself, as we know from the correspondence, delighted in "designating" dovecotes, stables and similar rural outbuildings (Arch. of the State of Mantua, Arch. Gonzaga, 2100, 10.II.70) and sometimes designed much la
2 CONC rger buildings, such as the palace of Revere. Quickly summarizing the main interventions desired by L. III in the territory, we know that starting from 1447 the Gonzaga architects and engineers began to work on the castle of Rever
2 CONC e; from the mid-fifties Bertola da Novate and Giovanni da Padova were engaged in the construction of the Goito canal and the governolo lock; also in 1461, major renovation works had begun on the fortress of Cavriana and on the for
2 CONC tifications of Goito; starting from 1468 work was done on the palace of Gonzaga and between 1470 and 1475 on the residence of Saviola.
2 CONT
2 CONT Starting from the end of the fifties (with the sole exception of the construction of the new city hospital, in 1450), partly due to, partly following the Diet which revealed in the eyes of L. III the partial inadequacy of Mantua c
2 CONC ompared to the model of the ideal city embodied by Florence, L. III implemented an impressive series of interventions on the urban fabric.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1459 the construction site of S. Sebastiano began on a project presented by Leon Battista Alberti in Mantua on the occasion of the Diet, and Donatello was urged to complete an ark dedicated to s. Anselm, patron saint of the cit
2 CONC y, to whom he had been working for some years; in 1460 Alberti proposed a reorganization project for the rotunda of S. Lorenzo; between 1459 and 1461 the project by Antonio Ciaccheri for S. Andrea was elaborated; between 1462 and 
2 CONC 1464 the Palazzo del Podestà was restructured; in 1470 Alberti prepared the project for S. Andrea, later chosen by L. III; between 1470 and 1473 the area of ​​the now dilapidated market house was rearranged and the clock tower was
2 CONC  erected; at the end of the fifties the central squares of the city were paved over.civitas vetus and the new Renaissance pole represented by S. Sebastiano. The highest point of the renovatio urbis is represented by the rebuilding
2 CONC  of the urban Benedictine monastery of S. Andrea. Once the monastery was transformed into a provost of the Gonzaga patronage, the first stone of the new building, intended to house the relics of the Holy Vessels of Christ, was lai
2 CONC d on 12 June 1472.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the same time, the prince's attention was also directed to the residence, at this time consisting of the two contiguous blocks of the Palazzo del Capitano and the Magna Domus , to which the castle of S. Giorgio was added toward
2 CONC s the lake at the end of the fourteenth century, built by Bartolino of Novara.
2 CONT
2 CONT Already in 1458 in particular the latter was the subject of profound renovations since L. III, in anticipation of the arrival of Pius II who was left with the central body of the buildings, decided to move there. Already in his fi
2 CONC rst years of marquisate, L. III had also followed up on the iconographic programs undertaken by his father, and in particular on the Arthurian cycle imagined by Pisanello in the palace; but a tangible change in taste due to his st
2 CONC ay in Florence on the occasion of the Florentine conduct of 1447 (when L. III took over the building of the tribune of the Ss. Annunziata) prompted the marquis in the early 1460s to entrust Andrea Mantegna with the task of frescoi
2 CONC ng the rooms of the castle, destined again to the residence of the lord.
2 CONT
2 CONT An exchange of letters had been going on between L. III and Mantegna since 1456 which led the Paduan painter to move permanently to Mantua in 1460. In the history of the relationship between the marquis and the painter, the peculi
2 CONC ar place occupied by the cycle of frescoes in the so-called of the spouses, so to speak, pushes into the background what in reality should be read as an articulated and lasting history of commissioning, the fruits of which (destin
2 CONC ed both for the city residence and for the palaces of the countryside), not always preserved, are however very well testified by contemporary sources. Mantegna probably first had to take care of the private chapel of the princes i
2 CONC n the castle, for which, according to the most recent studies, he had to paint a decorative cycle on panels of which we have The Death of the Virginconserved in the Prado Museum in Madrid and probably the plates ( Epiphany , Circu
2 CONC mcision , Ascension) that make up the triptych of the Uffizi in Florence, then, starting with certainty from 1465 (but a hint contained in the engagement deed between Federico Gonzaga and Margherita of Wittelsbach drawn up on 8 Se
2 CONC ptember 1462 could predate the start of the works) until at least 1474, of the Painted Room, better known as the Wedding Room. The exceptional nature of the castle's decorative cycle justifies the amount of studies that have been 
2 CONC dedicated both to its composition phases and to its interpretation. Beyond the different and various interpretations of episodes, dates and characters, it was a pictorial intervention of extraordinary intentionality, whose meaning
2 CONC  of affirmation, political and humanistic, achieved by the Mantuan dynasty stands out unequivocally: the family of L. III in its different dignities,
2 CONT
2 CONT The pacts with Milan of 1459 resumed the terms of 1454 for another three years: these were the years of the war for the succession of Ferdinand of Aragon to his father Alfonso on the throne of Naples, a war in which Sforza partici
2 CONC pated alongside the Aragonese against the French party of René and Charles of Anjou. L. III, despite his position of pre-eminence among the leaders, did everything possible not to participate personally in hostilities: in this sen
2 CONC se, a significant recognition of L. III's less and less military role in the Sforza system was that he was created, in conduct of 1459, lieutenant general of the Duchy of Milan.
2 CONT
2 CONT The lieutenancy, with the civil powers over the Milanese state that it entailed, gave the Mantuan conduct, and therefore the relationship between the Marquis of Mantua and the dukes, a peculiar and political trait of safeguarding 
2 CONC and internal consolidation of the Duchy, the importance of which would emerge in particularly in the difficult moments of Duke Francesco's illness or in the successions of 1466 and 1476.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1463 L. III renewed the Milanese conduct, but withdrew from his office shortly after also due to the breakdown of the negotiations between the Mantuan and Sforza courts regarding the planned marriage between Galeazzo Maria Sfor
2 CONC za and Dorotea Gonzaga.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the first Milanese contract of 1450 it had been decided that Sforza's eldest son, Galeazzo Maria, should marry a daughter of L. III, Susanna, born in 1447. In the renewal of the contract of 1454 the clause had been reconfirmed 
2 CONC and, when in 1457 Susanna showed the first signs of a spinal malformation, the first daughter of L. III was replaced without difficulty by her sister Dorotea, born in 1449. When, starting from 1461, Francesco Sforza began to consi
2 CONC der the opportunity of a more prestigious marriage for the eldest son, in the wake of a series of proposals aired by the French king Louis XI, doubts were also advanced by the Milanese court on Dorothea's physical suitability: in 
2 CONC 1463 L. III therefore broke off the negotiations. In that same year, however, another marriage event came to fruition, namely the union of the eldest son of L. III, Frederick, with Margaret of Wittelsbach, daughter of Albert II th
2 CONC e Pious, later Duke of Bavaria, distantly related to Barbara of Brandenburg. The wedding was celebrated on 7 June 1463 and, together with the marriages of Barbara with Eberardo I duke of Württemberg in 1474 and of the youngest dau
2 CONC ghter, Paola, with Leonardo count of Gorizia in 1477, completed the picture of the Germanic alliances of the Gonzagas, reinforcing a thick web of relations with the German Principalities and with the Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of Francesco Sforza, which took place on 8 March 1466, although tempted by an attractive Venetian offer, L. III ratified a three-year contract with Ferdinand of Aragon, thanks to whose explicit mediation, he, in th
2 CONC e latter's pay , undertook to operate in defense of the Duchy of Milan according to the clauses of the previous conducts. Sforza's death had in fact made it once more necessary for the new duke Galeazzo Maria to control the Gonzag
2 CONC a pawn on the eastern borders. L. III therefore appeared as lieutenant of the Aragonese in Lombardy and as a member and associate of the Kingdom, the Duchy and the Serenissima in the renewal of the league in 1468: in 1470 the pact
2 CONC s were stipulated directly with Galeazzo Maria, in the usual terms.
2 CONT
2 CONT This last renewal of the conduct was characterized, by express wish of the marquis, by a high level of ceremonial formalism, made necessary according to L. III by the damage suffered to his reputation in the bitter affair of the t
2 CONC ermination of Dorotea's marriage contract, as well as by a long series of real or presumed Milanese shortcomings regarding the obligations contracted by the dukes through the contracts stipulated since 1450; at the renewal in 1472
2 CONC  a proper conduct was also foreseen for Federico Gonzaga, eldest son of the marquis. In 1474 L. III figured in the renewed pact of alliance between Milan, Florence and Venice, which he signed in Mantua on 18 November, as adherent 
2 CONC of the Duke of Milan.
2 CONT
2 CONT L. III's position in the assembly of the Italian states was now consolidated. When in the summer of 1476 Niccolò d'Este, son of the Marquis Leonello and Margherita Gonzaga, sister of L. III - who had taken refuge at the Mantuan co
2 CONC urt since August 1471 due to the succession to the Este domains of his uncle Ercole - tried again to seizing Ferrara with a coup d'état, apparently with the support of L. III, he claimed his own extraneousness to the facts: his go
2 CONC od faith was believed and the death by decapitation of his nephew did not compromise relations between Mantua and Ferrara. Shortly thereafter, on the other hand, L. III had to deal with matters of much greater weight: in December 
2 CONC 1476, in fact, Galeazzo Maria Sforza was assassinated and L. III, although in precarious health conditions, he mobilized his troops on the borders with the Duchy and personally rushed to Milan on 6 January. 1477, contributing with
2 CONC  his presence to ensure the succession of Gian Galeazzo Maria under the regency of the Duchess Bona of Savoy. L. III's authority and prestige were now out of the question: in March 1477 the marquis obtained the honor of the Golden
2 CONC  Rose from Pope Sixtus IV. Since, as mentioned, he was in poor physical condition at the end of the summer of that year, he went to the spa to treat the flare-up of leg problems, of which we have the first news starting from 1468.
2 CONC  III were now out of the question: in March 1477 the Marquis obtained the honor of the Golden Rose from Pope Sixtus IV. Since, as mentioned, he was in poor physical condition at the end of the summer of that year, he went to the s
2 CONC pa to treat the flare-up of leg problems, of which we have the first news starting from 1468. III were now out of the question: in March 1477 the Marquis obtained the honor of the Golden Rose from Pope Sixtus IV. Since, as mention
2 CONC ed, he was in poor physical condition at the end of the summer of that year, he went to the spa to treat the flare-up of leg problems, of which we have the first news starting from 1468.
2 CONT
2 CONT L. III had a long-lasting tradition with spas: in fact he had gone to the baths at least seven times between 1438 and 1477, preferring the Sienese station of Petriolo. During his stay in 1477 he had the opportunity to settle a dis
2 CONC pute between Lucca and Genoa regarding the castle of Pietrasanta as arbitrator.
2 CONT
2 CONT A plague that broke out in Mantua in May 1478 prompted L. III to take refuge in the castle of Goito, while the marquise with her children went on June 1st to the suburban village of San Giorgio: from there she rushed to Goito a fe
2 CONC w days later due to illness On 11 June Barbara wrote to her children to join her, because L. III's conditions had suddenly worsened: on 12 June 1478 the Marquis died in Goito, not of the plague but probably of pleurisy.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around L. III's last wishes some shadows remain: although according to Schivenoglia he had already made a will in 1472, the deed was not found and the Marquise Barbara, claiming to know its contents, proceeded with her children to
2 CONC  divide the Marquisate by separating from the central trunk, intended for the eldest son Federico, the prerogatives for the cadets Francesco, Gianfrancesco, Rodolfo and Ludovico, in a division determined by the desire not to creat
2 CONC e dangerous resentments between the children. It was a dismemberment which, contrary to the one accomplished thirty years earlier by the Marquis Gianfrancesco, would prove to be definitive. If on the one hand it may seem strange t
2 CONC hat L. III would once again divide his state,Arch. Gonzaga , b. 2185, Gianfrancesco Gonzaga to Matteo Corradi, 7 Jan. 1443), on the other hand, the sudden death of L. III and the rapidity of the actions of Federico and his brother
2 CONC s suggest that it was not a matter of an improvised plan in a few days by the marquise and her heirs against the will of Ludovico III.
2 CONT
2 CONT L. III was buried in the cathedral of Mantua, in all probability in the chapel dedicated to S. Francesco. He left behind him the memory of a prince skilled in arms, cultured and prudent, but above all upright and loyal, who placed
2 CONC  his honour, his loyalty to his commitments and the good of his family and his city above all considerations. In the history of the Gonzaga domination of Mantua, the more than thirty years of his principality represented, after si
2 CONC xty years of convulsive war uprisings, a long phase of stability and an institutional and social construction whose depth is well reflected in the urban image that still distinguishes the city in a distinctive way."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------
