1 NAME Paolo /di Sangro/
2 GIVN Paolo
2 SURN di Sangro
2 _MARNM di Sangro
1 NAME Paolo di Sangro, signore di Torremaggiore
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1455
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Paolo di Sangro (d. 1455) was an Italian nobleman and condottiere, lord of Agnone, Atessa, Civitacampomarano, San Severo, and Torremaggiore.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coat of arms of the De Sangro family
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Paolo was born on an unknown date and place to Cola Tommaso di Sangro and Maria Gianvilla, and was the eldest of their children (he had three brothers, Antonio, Carlo, and Princivalle, and two sisters, Beatrice and Constance).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He embarked on a military career in the mercenary army of Jacopo Caldora, eventually becoming captain and participating in various campaigns between the Angevins and the Aragonese, who claimed the throne of the Kingdom of Naples. 
2 CONC On November 15, 1439, he attended Caldora's funeral in the church of Santo Spirito al Morrone in Sulmona, after Caldora had died suddenly during the siege of Colle Sannita. He then entered military service under Antonio Caldora's 
2 CONC son and followed him through his frequent changes of allegiance.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Paolo di Sangro achieved fame at the Battle of Sessano on June 28, 1442, where he defected to Alfonso V of Aragon, sealing Antonio Caldora's defeat and heralding the end of his family's rule in the Kingdom of Naples.[3] As a rewar
2 CONC d for his victory, the Aragonese granted him the fiefdom of Torremaggiore and a large sum of money.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT n August of that same year, he defeated the condottieri Cesare Martinengo, Vittore Rangoni, and Lionello Accrocciamuro at Troia. There he met Antonio Caldora (who had since switched sides again), who tasked him with persuading Alf
2 CONC onso V of Aragon (who had by then become ruler of the kingdom) to return the Duchy of Bari to Caldora from the Prince of Tarentum, Giovanni Antonio Orsini del Balzo.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In October 1443, he marched against Francesco Sforza and came to the aid of the mercenary captain Niccolò Piccinino in the Marches. In November, after leaving Montegiorgio, he captured Torre San Patrizio after a three-day siege an
2 CONC d burned it down; later, with the support of the inhabitants of Sant’Elpidio a Mare, he occupied Monte Urano. In December, however, he had to abandon the campaign in the Marca Fermana due to a lack of funds and supplies.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In August 1444, he crushed the rebellion of Antonio Centelles Ventimiglia, Marquis of Crotone, with an army of 1,000 knights. In December of the same year, he encamped with Giacomo Montagano at Macerata. In 1448, he participated i
2 CONC n the victorious Battle of Piombino against the Republic of Florence with an army of 2,500 knights. In June 1452, he marched with 600 knights from L'Aquila alongside Ferrante of Aragon against the Florentines. In 1454, after the P
2 CONC eace of Lodi, the Republic of Florence offered him the position of Captain-General of the Florentine army with a salary of 30,000 ducats. King Alfonso V of Aragon forced him to decline the offer and instead granted him the fiefs o
2 CONC f Agnone, Atessa, and San Severo.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Paolo di Sangro died in 1455.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Descendants
2 CONT Paolo di Sangro married Abenante Attendolo, the daughter of Domenico, brother of the famous mercenary leader Muzio Attendolo Sforza, and Giovannella Gesualdo. With her he had:[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Carlo, the eldest son, inherited his father's estate and married Caterina Caetani, with whom he had Gianfrancesco.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Alfonso, Lord of Dogliola, Fresagrandinaria, and Palmoli, who married Antonia Caracciolo.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferraguto, Lord of Lentella.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Altobella, who married Cola di Monforte-Gambatesa on November 21, 1450.[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT Emilia, who married Giulio della Leonessa.[9]
2 CONT
